From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 11:11:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] uninstall: Codex's config gives up what magpie wrote even
 with the marks gone

adamzmsun uninstalled magpie and Codex kept failing every turn: its
config.toml still had openai_base_url on magpie's gateway, a port
nothing answered any more.

Uninstalling disconnects the agents magpie is in, and the failover's
own end takes the base URL out first. Both name what magpie wrote by
the marks in magpie's own files (codex.joined, codex.beside,
codex.failover): with those gone -- the folder deleted by hand before
uninstalling, a fresh install beside an old config.toml, a magpie from
before the marks existed -- Codex reads as not connected (Wired wants a
mark or a magpie model, and a base URL beside one of Codex's own models
is neither, #1385), and failover says no (FailingOver wants its mark),
so nothing named the base URL, and it stayed: every turn a connection
failure that says nothing of magpie (the issue's http_connection_failed
after some fifty seconds). magpie as Codex's provider with the marks
gone was left the same way, model_provider and all.

Now uninstalling ends with a leftovers pass: the base URL naming
magpie's gateway and magpie as its provider go by the same gates a
Disconnect takes them by (dropBase by viaBase, dropProvider by
asProvider), marks or not -- once magpie is gone for good, nothing
serves that address, whoever wrote it there. Codex's own model,
provider and keys stay, and so does magpie's provider table, whose
threads started on magpie name it; the pass is this machine's Codex,
like EndCodexFailover. Taking something restarts the Codex app's
app-server, which read the old config as it started.

Fixes #3866
